A||N||6||burdell1||Passenger Side Door Handle Repair||||1||
Z||000000||burdell1||02-08-2000||03:32 PM||||My passenger side outside door handle has recently started breaking. Specifically, the left side has came loose while the right side is still attached and works. Does this sound like it can be saved? Or should I start looking for a replacement one?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-08-2000||04:32 PM||||As I recall that handle is common to several GM models. The thing is a pretty big pain to replace. And yeah, you pretty much have to replace it when it breaks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||10:10 PM||||Pull the inner door panel off, then the mirror, then the outer panel.  Make sure you remove the retaining clips for the lock cylinder and door handle.  The door lock and handle will come off with the outer panel.  It's fairly easy to do once the skin is off.  Here are some pics showing what to do.<BR>1st is a pic of the skin before you remove anything.  Note the framework which must be removed.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/doorhandleassy.JPG"> <BR>Next is the skin minus the frame.  You have to remove the two flimsy nuts to get the frame off.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/doorhandle-noframe.JPG"> <BR>Here's the frame just to make sure you see what needs to be removed.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/doorhandle-frame.JPG"> <BR>Here's the bare skin and handle.  One of the holes for the two bolts (which stay attached to the handle assy) is oblong, but the other is a real *****  to get the bolt loose from.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/doorskin-bare.JPG">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/doorhandle.JPG"> <BR>Sorry about the bandwidth, but I think pics make a better story than just words.  Besides, I might as well get some use out of my camera.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 02-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-10-2000||10:08 PM||||Taking the outer door skin off is a pain in the hinny and is not necessary (unless you have really big paws that don't fit into the inner door area). The inner panel does have to be removed. You'll need to remove the trim off the outer door handle and put it on the new handle (I used automotive emblem double-sided tape). The handles are standard GM style that you can find in the red packaged "Help" section of most parts stores. Careful with the plastic clip, I broke one of those and had to buy another one too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||burdell1||02-23-2000||10:50 AM||||Could it be that a rod that connects to the door handle came loose? is there an easier way to check that without taking the inside off? Could a person just take the black outside trim off and get at it that way?<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-23-2000||05:05 PM||||Ummm...No, not that I know of, and no, again.<BR>The rod that you're referring to has nothing to do with the handle being loose.  There are 2 nuts holding the handle assy on.  (See above pics)  Even if you take the trim off, you still can't get to anything useful.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||02-23-2000||09:36 PM||||You have to take the inner door panel off to get to the trim clips anyway, so that won't do you any good. Usually the little part on the handle that the rod attaches to is what breaks. However, there is an easy way to check and see if the rod came loose. Open the door and look at the top of the back edge of the door above the latch near the window. You'll see a odd shaped rubber cover attached by one screw. Take this off and you can shine a light in there to see what's happening. Removing the inner door panel is not hard, once you know the little tricks. I can pull one off in about 10 minutes or less.||||reg||1
